The imagination horizon $H$ sets the number of rollout steps. We test $H{=}15$ (default) and $H{=}8$. The reduced horizon controls for compounding error: $H{=}8$ steps at $\dt{=}2$ covers $16$ physical time steps, comparable to the $15$ physical steps of the default configuration. Additionally, the parameter \texttt{gate\_fixed\_dt} forces all imagination steps to use a specific $\dt$ value ($1$, $2$, or $4$), separating the effect of $\dt$-conditioning from any learned gating policy.

\subsection{Factorial Design}
\label{sec:factorial}

We adopt a structured factorial ablation across four factors: $\dt$-embedding mode (trained / untrained / none), temporal subsampling (on / off), imagination horizon ($H{=}15$ / $H{=}8$), and imagination step size ($\dt{=}1$, $2$, or $4$). Table~\ref{tab:factorial_design} summarizes the nine configurations.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Factorial design. Each row specifies one experimental configuration. \textbf{Emb}: $\dt$-embedding mode. \textbf{Sub}: temporal subsampling. \textbf{H}: imagination horizon. \textbf{Imag $\dt$}: temporal resolution used during imagination.}
\label{tab:factorial_design}
\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3.5pt}
\begin{tabular}{@{}llcccc@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{ID} & \textbf{Purpose} & \textbf{Emb} & \textbf{Sub} & \textbf{H} & \textbf{Imag $\dt$} \\
\midrule
001 & Reference baseline & --- & off & 15 & 1 \\
006 & Fixed $\dt{=}2$ & trn & off & 15 & 2 \\
007 & Fixed $\dt{=}4$ & trn & off & 15 & 4 \\
005 & Learned gate & trn & on & 15 & learned \\
\midrule
008d & Full treatment & trn & on & 8 & 2 \\
008f & Isolate emb & --- & on & 8 & 2 \\
008a & Isolate subsample & utr & off & 15 & 2 \\
008c & Isolate horizon & utr & off & 8 & 2 \\
\midrule
008e\_v3 & Mitigation ($\dt{=}1$ imag) & trn & on & 15 & 1 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\vspace{-2pt}
{\raggedright\footnotesize trn = trained, utr = untrained (frozen random projection), --- = removed.\par}
\end{table}

The design targets three diagnostic questions. First, comparing 008d (trained embedding) against 008f (no embedding) with all other factors held constant isolates whether \emph{training} the temporal conditioning triggers pathological dynamics. Second, comparing 008a and 008c (both untrained, differing only in $H$) against 008d (trained) separates embedding training from horizon and subsampling effects. Third, the mitigation configuration 008e\_v3 retains the trained embedding for observation conditioning but restricts imagination to $\dt{=}1$, testing whether constraining the imagination channel alone prevents collapse.

Each non-baseline configuration is run with 2--3 seeds. The baseline uses 2 seeds (seeds 1 and 2) to establish reference variance; a third seed (seed 0) is in progress. Following \citet{agarwal2021precipice}, we report per-seed trajectories rather than aggregated means to surface stochastic divergence across seeds.

\subsection{Diagnostic Metrics}
\label{sec:metrics}

\paragraph{Evaluation return.}
The primary performance metric: mean return over 10 evaluation episodes, computed every 10K training steps with a deterministic policy.

\paragraph{Value-to-eval ratio.}
We define the value-to-eval ratio $v/e$ as the ratio of the critic's mean imagined value to the evaluation return at each checkpoint. This tracks how well value estimates match realized performance. A ratio near~1 indicates calibrated value predictions; values substantially above~1 indicate systematic overestimation.

\paragraph{Normalized value-to-eval ratio ($\nve$).}
To control for the baseline's natural $v/e$ dynamics (\eg, the ratio naturally increases during early training as the critic learns), we normalize:
\begin{equation}
\nve_t = \frac{(v/e)_{\text{config},\,t}}{(v/e)_{\text{baseline},\,t}}
\label{eq:nve}
\end{equation}
where $(v/e)_{\text{baseline},\,t}$ is from the single baseline seed (exp\_001, seed~1, 505K steps).  We restrict to a \textbf{35K+ window} ($t \geq 35\text{K}$) because the baseline $v/e$ is near zero in early training, causing spurious $\nve$ inflation.  We use $\nve \geq 1.5\times$ (peak within this window) as a warning threshold; across 8 evaluable seeds, this achieves 7/8 accuracy (AUROC${=}0.933$), with one non-CIVO false positive (\S\ref{sec:f6}).  The threshold is sensitive but not perfectly specific to CIVO; mechanistic attribution requires joint analysis with collapse signatures (\S\ref{sec:pathways}).

\paragraph{RSSM fidelity.}
To probe the mechanism underlying value drift, we evaluate world model prediction quality at different temporal scales from the same checkpoint. Specifically, we compute observation MSE and reward MAE on held-out $\dt{=}1$ versus $\dt{=}2$ transitions, and report the gap ratio between them. A large gap indicates that the world model produces systematically different prediction quality across temporal scales---providing the channel for critic exploitation.

\paragraph{Supplementary diagnostics.}
Two additional metrics target failure modes that $\nve$ may miss.
\emph{Actor loss sign}: DreamerV3's actor loss is normally negative (policy improvement direction). A sign flip to positive values directly signals that the critic is misleading the actor, providing a more targeted marker of delusional-critic damage than the aggregate $\nve$ ratio.
\emph{Value standard deviation}: exponential growth in value variance across imagination rollouts captures oscillatory instability during ``false recovery'' phases where $\nve$ may temporarily decrease even as the underlying value landscape destabilizes.

\subsection{Environment and Training Details}
\label{sec:env_details}

All experiments use the Walker Walk task from the DeepMind Control Suite \citep{tassa2018dmcontrol} with proprioceptive observations. The baseline runs for 500K environment steps, sufficient for convergence (peak return 970.4). Treatment configurations run for 105K steps, which is sufficient to observe both collapse onset and the diagnostic markers described above. All DreamerV3 hyperparameters follow the defaults from \citet{hafner2023dreamerv3} without task-specific tuning. Training uses a single NVIDIA RTX 6000 Ada GPU with JIT compilation disabled (\texttt{-{}-compile False}) for reproducibility across runs. The codebase extends DreamerV3 with the variable-rate modules described in \S\ref{sec:variable_rate}; no other architectural modifications are made.

\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

We organize results around six findings (F1--F6) that establish a causal chain from trained temporal conditioning to pathological value dynamics and performance collapse. All experiments use Walker Walk (DMC) with DreamerV3; treatments run to 105K steps and the baseline to 505K steps. We report evaluation returns (mean over 10 episodes) and the normalized value-to-eval ratio ($\nve$), defined as the ratio of a configuration's imagined value to evaluation return, normalized by the baseline's ratio at the same training step.

